FCK=[('Hazards',['Fixed wiring inspected and tested at least every 5 years (EICR)','Electrical appliances checked regularly, and visually between lets','Trailing leads and adaptors avoided where possible','Gas appliances checked and serviced every 12 months by a Gas Safe registered engineer','Smoking and vaping policy set, with guest instructions','Heating and hot water serviced every year; portable heaters avoided','Open fire or log burner: spark guard, chimney swept yearly, metal ash bin, guest instructions','Carbon monoxide alarm wherever there is a gas or solid fuel appliance','No candles, tea lights or ethanol burners provided for guests','Bins and combustibles kept clear of the building, especially windows','Furniture meets the Furniture and Furnishings (Fire) (Safety) Regulations 1988 (check labels)']),
     ('Escape and alarms',['Escape routes (hall, stairs) clear of storage','Exit doors open from inside without a key (latch or thumb turn)','No guest bedroom reached only through another room, unless on the ground floor with a direct exit or escape window','Doors onto the stairs and hall are solid, well-fitting doors (hollow or thin-panel doors replaced with 30-minute fire doors)','Interlinked smoke alarms in hall, landing, living rooms and bedrooms','Heat alarm in the kitchen (and utility or laundry room)','Alarms mains powered with a sealed backup battery (Grade D1); sealed 10-year battery alarms (Grade F1) only as a stop-gap','Torch in each bedroom, or plug-in night lights on the stairs and hall, if escape lighting is not fitted','Fire blanket and extinguisher in the kitchen (optional for self-catering, but common)']),
     ('Paperwork and guests',['Written fire risk assessment, specific to this property, recorded and kept','Name of whoever did the assessment recorded (if you used an assessor)','Fire action notice and escape plan for guests (back of bedroom door or welcome pack)','Exact address and postcode, and how to call 999, given to guests','Smoke and heat alarms tested at every changeover and at least monthly','Testing and maintenance log kept (paper or digital)','Assessment reviewed at least yearly, and after any change or fire','Fire safety information ready to hand over if someone else takes on the property'])]
FFAQ=[('Do holiday lets in England need a fire risk assessment?','Yes. If you charge someone to stay and the property is not being occupied as a private dwelling, the Regulatory Reform (Fire Safety) Order 2005 applies, even for one night. You must carry out a suitable and sufficient fire risk assessment and record the findings.'),
      ('Can I do the fire risk assessment myself?','For small, simple properties, yes, if you feel able to. The Home Office guide is written so most owners can do it without specialist knowledge. If you are not confident, use a competent fire risk assessor. You stay legally responsible either way.'),
      ('Do I need to write the fire risk assessment down?','Yes. You must keep a record of the completed assessment, including its findings and anyone you identify as especially at risk. The guide says you should also record who carried it out.'),
      ('What smoke alarms does a holiday let need?','The guide recommends interlinked smoke alarms in hallways, landings, living rooms and bedrooms, and a heat alarm in the kitchen. They should be mains powered with a backup battery (Grade D1). Sealed long-life battery alarms (Grade F1) may be accepted for around 2 to 3 years until D1 alarms are fitted.'),
      ('Do I need fire doors in a holiday cottage?','Not usually full fire doors with closers. In a typical two-storey house or cottage, solid, well-fitting doors onto the hall and stairs are normally acceptable. Hollow-core or thin-panel doors, and plain glazing in doors, will normally need replacing with 30-minute fire doors.'),
      ('Do I have to provide a fire extinguisher?','Not for guests in self-catering lets. The guide says guests should not be expected to fight fires, but you may wish to provide a small extinguisher and fire blanket in the kitchen. If staff are on site or visit regularly, firefighting equipment should be provided and staff trained.'),
      ('How often should I test the alarms?','Domestic smoke and heat alarms should be tested at least monthly. In self-catering lets where you are not present, do the weekly and monthly checks at every changeover.'),
      ('Who enforces fire safety in holiday lets?','Your local fire and rescue service. It may inspect your property and can give advice, but it cannot do the risk assessment for you.'),
      ('Does this guide apply in Scotland, Wales or Northern Ireland?','No. The Home Office guide applies only to England. Wales has its own guidance, and Scotland and Northern Ireland have different (but similar) fire safety laws.')]
